# Standing-rules starter template

An agent-agnostic starter for the standing-rules / context file the council injects
in front of every member. Install it one of two ways:

- Copy it to `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (the path the council reads by default). If your
  access method is Claude Code, that same path is also auto-loaded into the leader
  every session (code.claude.com/docs/en/memory.md, checked 2026-07-23) -- so rename
  this file to `CLAUDE.md` and place it there.
- Or keep any name/location and set `COUNCIL_STANDING_RULES_PATH` to this file's path
  -- the council reads it there, no `CLAUDE.md` required (the fully agent-agnostic option).

## Why this file matters to the council

The council READS this file and puts it in front of every member. Bar item 12 makes
your standing instructions binding, so a member can cite the exact rule a proposal
broke instead of inferring one.

To be precise about what you lose without it: bar item 12 ALSO covers the
directives you type during a session, which the council extracts from the
transcript and enforces whether or not this file exists. So the council is not
toothless without a standing-rules file. What is missing is the STANDING half -- the rules
that hold across every session and that you should not have to restate.

The block is placed AFTER the evidence in a member's prompt, on purpose. Part of
this file ends up being written by the agent about itself, and a reviewer who reads
the reviewed party's account of its own defects before reading a single fact has been
framed.

## READ THIS BEFORE COPYING IT

Section 2 below is NOT a set of universal truths about language models. It is a
catalogue of failures observed on ONE project, across 7,380 council-reviewed file
edits. Whether they are also your agent's failures, on your codebase, is something
we did not measure. Treat section 2 as a STARTING POINT and expect to replace much
of it.

Section 3 is the loop that produced section 2. NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS MEASURED,
and we are flagging that rather than dressing it up: in this project the loop felt
like the part that carried over, and copied-in rules seemed to get ignored while
rules added after watching a failure twice seemed to stick. That is an impression
from one place. Test it against your own work instead of taking it from us.

