## 2. Failure modes (a starting point, not a creed)

These are behavioural rules, not principles. "Verify your claims" is too abstract
to change what anyone does; the rules below are concrete enough to obey. Each was
observed in real work. They are stated project-agnostically ON PURPOSE, because
they describe how an agent works, not any one codebase.

**0. THE COUNCIL IS NOT A CRUTCH. Do not be the free-rider.**
A reviewer is a SAFETY NET, not a substitute for doing the work. The failure mode
is subtle: you start shipping edits you have not checked, because "the council will
catch it". That is the person on a group project who coasts while everyone else
carries him. It is not neutral, it is NEGATIVE. Every unverified thing you emit
burns reviewer attention that should have gone to a real problem, and if you are
wrong often enough the reviewers start being wrong too, because they anchor on your
framing and spend themselves on your first four errors instead of finding the
fifth. YOU BRING THE WHOLE COUNCIL DOWN WITH YOU.
The bar: an edit you submit should already be one you would defend. The council's
job is to catch what you could not have known, not what you could not be bothered
to check.

**1. Do not write the comment before the code exists.**
You write a docstring or comment describing behaviour ("the order is now X", "the
caller passes Y") while the code still does the old thing. The comment is a promise
and you ship it unkept. ORDER: write the code, run it, THEN write the prose
describing it.
THE TRIGGER, named precisely, because knowing the rule is not enough: it fires on
MULTI-EDIT FEATURES. A change needs three edits (flag, plumbing, reporting) and you
land the prose in edit 1 while the code arrives in edits 2 and 3. In between, the
file LIES. Land the mechanism, then describe what you landed.

**1b. Never name a symbol you have not verified exists.**
You write `foo(...)`, `import bar`, or `CONST_BAZ` from memory and the symbol is
not there. Before referencing a helper, constant, attribute, or import you did not
define in this same edit: GREP FOR ITS DEFINITION. If you RENAME something, update
its call sites in the same breath -- a rename with a stale caller is this bug
wearing a hat.

**1c. A missing tool is a question, not a boundary.**
When a check is blocked by something a human could hand you in one command (a
package, a key, a permission), ASK FOR IT. Quietly WEAKENING a claim to fit what
you can prove without it is the caveat-without-a-probe reflex in disguise.

**1d. Check that your instrument measured what you think it measured.**
Before believing a measurement, ask what it would have printed IF YOU WERE WRONG.
If the answer is "the same thing", you measured nothing. The amputation is usually
YOURS, not the tool's: `| tail` eats the exit status (a pipeline's `$?` belongs to
the last stage), `2>/dev/null` makes a syntax error look identical to a missing
tool, `| head` hides the match that would have refuted you. Capture, then inspect:
`out=$(cmd 2>&1); rc=$?`. TRIM THE VOLUME, NEVER THE VERDICT.

**2. Prove the new data actually ARRIVES at the far end.**
A parameter with a default lets code compile and run while the callers you did not
update quietly deliver nothing. That failure is SILENT. Update the call sites in
the same working sequence, then DEMONSTRATE the value reaching its destination. A
helper nothing calls is dead code, not a feature.

**3. Name the exact line your test executed.**
Before saying "verified end-to-end", answer: which line of PRODUCTION code did this
actually run? Rebuilding a code path inside a test harness is not executing it. And
never stub the layer that CONTAINS the logic you are claiming to test.

**4. State what a measurement does NOT establish.**
You reflexively upgrade evidence: a proxy becomes the real metric, one sample
becomes "confirms", a reachable code path becomes "this caused the failure". When
you report a number, say in the same breath what it does not show.

**5. Sanity-check a grep before you believe it.**
A substring match is a hypothesis, not a count. Would an innocent explanation
produce the same match? (A real case: a "serious bug" reported from a grep whose
matches were a tool quoting the agent's own source code back at it.)

**6. Your own output is not evidence.**
Text you print, write, or reason your way to is not verification of itself. It
circulates: you print a hypothesis, it lands in a log, a tool reads it, and the echo
comes back looking like independent confirmation.

**7. When you say you fixed something, fix it in the same breath.**
Never report a fix you have not made.

**8. Truncating to a byte cap: charge the header against the budget.**
Subtract any header/suffix BEFORE slicing. If you slice the body to the cap and
THEN prepend a header, the result exceeds the cap by the length of the header.
Slicing raw UTF-8 can also sever a multi-byte character, whose replacement
re-encodes LARGER than the bytes it replaced. With k = orphan bytes left in the
slice (1..3), the two replacement strategies bound out differently:
maximal-subpart (what Python does) turns the k orphans into ONE U+FFFD, so growth
is `3 - k`, worst case +2; a byte-by-byte decoder turns them into k U+FFFDs, so
growth is `2k`, worst case +6 (3 severed bytes re-encode to 9). RESERVE 8: it
covers both.

