## 3. The self-correction loop

This is the part that generalises. Everything above is disposable; this is not.

WHEN A FAILURE REPEATS, AMEND THIS FILE.

- Not for one-off mistakes. For PATTERNS: the same class of error, twice or more.
- Write it in the concrete, behavioural form above ("do X before Y"), never as a
  restatement of "be careful" or "verify things". An abstraction changes nothing.
- Do it in the MOMENT you notice, not at the end of a session where it will be lost.
- Keep it project-agnostic. Project-specific detail belongs in that project's own
  instructions file.
- If a rule here is wrong, or has stopped mattering, DELETE it. This is a working
  instrument, not a monument.

The council can point at the agent's errors, and it will. It cannot fix the agent's
habits. A reviewer catching the same mistake a fifth time is the reviewer doing the
agent's job. Encode the correction so it does not have to be caught a sixth time.

## A caution about section 2, restated because it matters

Handing a reviewer the reviewed party's own list of known failures invites the
reviewer to hunt only for what is on the list. The council's prompt says this
explicitly, and you should believe it: the most valuable finding is one nobody
predicted. This file is a floor, never a ceiling.
